        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Bikes</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these bikes. They were chosen from manufacturer product pages and manuals,
            membership terms, recall records and independent coverage, and the claims on this page are
            limited to what those sources support. Each pick had to be on sale under its current name,
            its basic figures had to agree between at least two sources, and it had to replace a Peloton
            Bike or Bike+ for at least one kind of rider: someone who wants Peloton's classes, someone who
            wants a screen-led bike with automatic resistance, or someone who wants a better ride.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recall checks.</strong> We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall
            database by brand, by manufacturer and in recall descriptions for every maker on this page —
            Schwinn, BowFlex and their owner Johnson Health Tech, NordicTrack and its parent iFIT, Keiser and
            Wahoo — and for Peloton, Echelon and Zwift. None of the six bikes is under a recall. iFIT's
            recent recalls cover NordicTrack and ProForm adjustable dumbbells and the NordicTrack RW900
            rower, not its bikes. Johnson Health Tech's cover BowFlex SelectTech dumbbells and commercial
            Matrix training cycles, not the IC4 or C6. The Schwinn-branded bicycle recalls on record are
            from Pacific Cycle, a separate company that makes Schwinn bicycles rather than Schwinn Fitness
            bikes. We found no recalls for Keiser, Wahoo, Echelon or Zwift.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What we left out, and why</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Echelon.</strong> Echelon's Connect bikes are the closest look-alikes to a Peloton, and on
            19 October its store listed the Connect EX-5 from $799.99, the EX-5s from $1,199.99 and the
            EX-8s from $1,899.99. OutdoorGearLab, cited on our exercise bike guide, reports that a software
            update limited Echelon bikes to working only when connected to Echelon's servers, which in
            practice ties the bike to Echelon's membership. A Peloton alternative that is more locked-in
            than Peloton is not much of an alternative, so it is discussed below rather than carded.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack S22i and S27i.</strong> These studio bikes were NordicTrack's long-running
            Peloton rivals, but on 19 October NordicTrack's US site listed only the X24, the S24 Studio Bike
            and a recumbent. The S24 ($1,299.99 on sale, with a 24-inch screen and 24 resistance levels)
            is a cheaper flat-road option; we carded the X24 because we could cross-check its figures with
            an independent review and could not do the same for the S24. If you find an S22i or S27i new,
            check whether it is old stock and what warranty it carries.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Zwift Ride.</strong> Zwift's smart frame, sold with a KICKR CORE 2 for $1,299.99 on
            Zwift's US store on 19 October, is an interesting smart bike for Zwift riders. Zwift lists a 146
            to 198 cm rider height range and 120 kg maximum rider weight, but we found no independent review
            to check those figures against, so it is mentioned here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Peloton's own bikes.</strong> This is a page of alternatives, but if you are comparing,
            Peloton's current Cross Training Bike was $1,695 and Cross Training Bike+ $2,695 on Peloton's
            site on 19 October, and neither is part of the recalls described below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="peloton-app" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Peloton App+ vs All-Access: What You Pay on Another Bike</h2>
<p>
            Peloton sells three memberships, and which one you need depends on what you ride. On 19 October
            2026, Peloton's site gave these prices:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>All-Access Membership, $44 a month.</strong> Required to use Peloton's content on
            Peloton's own bikes, treads and rowers. It covers everyone in the household on one Peloton
            product per category, with individual profiles, and includes App+.</li>
            <li><strong>App+, $24 a month.</strong> Unlimited classes on the Peloton App on your own
            phone, tablet or TV, including Bike, Tread and Row classes. This is the tier that makes sense on
            a Schwinn, BowFlex or Keiser.</li>
            <li><strong>App One, $12.99 a month.</strong> The full library is visible, but Peloton limits
            App One members to three cardio equipment classes a month — Bike, Tread or Row — with a class
            counted once you have been in it for 180 seconds. That rules it out for anyone who wants to ride
            most days.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Over three years, App+ comes to $864 (36 x $24) and All-Access to $1,584 (36 x $44), a
            difference of $720 from the membership alone. That is arithmetic from the prices above, not a
            quote, and Peloton's prices have changed before.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Setup</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Bike (19 Oct)</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Membership</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">3-year total (arithmetic)</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Schwinn IC4 + Peloton App+</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$899</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$24/mo</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,763</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Peloton Cross Training Bike + All-Access</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,695</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$44/mo</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,279</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack X24 + iFIT Pro</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$39/mo</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,404</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Peloton Cross Training Bike+ + All-Access</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,695</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$44/mo</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,279</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Keiser M3i + Peloton App+</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $2,985</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$24/mo (optional)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,849, or $2,985 with no app</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The totals leave out tax, delivery, shoes, mats and any accessories, and the Peloton bike
            prices are Peloton's list prices on the day, which it often discounts. They are there to show
            the shape of the decision: an IC4 with App+ costs about half as much over three years as
            Peloton's cheapest bike with All-Access.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What you give up on a non-Peloton bike</h3>
          <p>
            The app runs the classes; it does not run the bike. On a Schwinn or Keiser, the Peloton App
            cannot read or set the bike's resistance, so you follow the instructor's cues using the bike's
            own console, and depending on the bike and app version it may not record all your data, as our
            exercise bike guide explains. Resistance numbers also differ between brands — "level 40" on one
            bike is not "40" on another — which is why IC4 riders rely on conversion charts. If following
            a number on a leaderboard matters to you, a Peloton bike or a bike whose own platform controls
            resistance, like the X24 with iFIT, does that job; if you are happy to ride by feel, the app on
            a cheaper bike loses little.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="closed-platforms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Closed Platforms: Echelon, iFIT and Peloton</h2>
<p>
            Every screen bike is built around its maker's platform, and how tightly the bike is tied to it
            decides what you own if you stop paying.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Peloton.</strong> Peloton says an All-Access Membership is required to use its content
            on its bikes. Without it, a Peloton bike is a bike with a large screen that shows little.</li>
            <li><strong>Echelon.</strong> OutdoorGearLab reports that Echelon bikes now work only when
            connected to Echelon's servers, which goes further: the bike's usefulness depends on the
            company's servers being up and your membership being active. If Echelon changed its terms or
            its service, owners would have little recourse.</li>
            <li><strong>iFIT (NordicTrack).</strong> NordicTrack's site says a paid iFIT Pro membership is
            required with equipment purchase to access content and features, and that a free iFIT account,
            agreement to terms and Wi-Fi are required to operate the equipment. NordicTrack also says the
            X24 can be used in manual mode with a free iFIT account, controlling resistance and incline
            from the handlebars. On 19 October iFIT's site listed iFIT Pro at $39 a month and iFIT Train, for
            equipment without a built-in screen, at $15. Our
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">is iFIT worth it guide</a> and
            <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack without iFIT</a>
            explain what that means in practice.</li>
            <li><strong>Open bikes.</strong> The IC4, C6, Keiser and Wahoo bikes broadcast over Bluetooth (and
            on the Wahoo, ANT+ and Wi-Fi) to apps of your choice. You can switch from Peloton to Zwift or
            anything else without changing the bike.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of this makes a closed platform a bad buy; iFIT's automatic incline and resistance changes
            are a real feature that the open bikes cannot match. It means the membership is part of the
            machine, and you should be comfortable paying for it for the bike's life. Our
            <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT vs Peloton comparison</a>
            covers the two biggest platforms side by side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-peloton" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Peloton Recalls: What to Check on a Used Bike</h2>
<p>
            A used Peloton can look like the cheapest alternative of all. Before buying one, know its recall
            history, because two of its bikes have been recalled for the same fault.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Peloton Bike, model PL01 (May 2023).</strong> About 2.2 million bikes, sold from January
            2018 to May 2023, were recalled because the seat post can break and detach during use. The CPSC
            notice lists 35 reports of breakage and 13 injuries, including a fractured wrist. The remedy is
            a free replacement seat post. The model number is on the inside of the front fork, near the
            flywheel.</li>
            <li><strong>Original Series Bike+, model PL02, serial numbers beginning with "T" (November 2025).</strong>
            About 833,000 bikes sold in the US from January 2020 to April 2025 were recalled for the same
            seat-post hazard, after three reports of breakage, two with injuries from falls. The serial
            number is inside or behind the front fork or behind the flywheel.</li>
            <li><strong>PR70P pedals (October 2020).</strong> First-generation clip-in pedals fitted to about 27,000
            Peloton bikes sold from July 2013 to May 2016 were recalled because they could break. Peloton
            recommends replacing pedals annually anyway.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Both bike recalls were sold through Peloton, DICK'S Sporting Goods and Amazon, so plenty are in
            circulation. Ask the seller for the model and serial number, check them against the recall, and
            confirm the replacement seat post has been fitted before anyone rides it. Ask also whether the
            seller's membership can be cancelled cleanly, because you will need your own All-Access
            Membership to use the bike's content. Peloton's current Cross Training Bike and Bike+ are not
            part of either recall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buyers-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Peloton Alternative Buyer's Guide</h2>
<ol>
            <li><strong>Decide what you are replacing.</strong> If you love Peloton's instructors, keep them
            and change the bike: an IC4 or C6 with App+. If you want the screen-led experience with
            automatic resistance, change the platform: the X24 with iFIT. If you want a better ride, change
            the category: a Keiser, a Wahoo smart bike or a smart trainer.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the fit range.</strong> Households with very different riders should look at
            stated rider heights first. Schwinn's own pages disagree on the IC4's range, as our exercise
            bike guide notes; Keiser's 4 ft 10 in to 7 ft and Wahoo's 5 ft 0 in to 6 ft 4 in are stated
            plainly.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the pedals.</strong> Peloton bikes use Delta-compatible cleats, according to
            Garage Gym Reviews; the IC4 and C6 have dual-sided SPD and toe-cage pedals; the Wahoo KICKR BIKE
            PRO ships without pedals. If you already own cycling shoes, match the cleat or plan to change
            the pedals.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the weight rating.</strong> The X24 and Keiser are rated to 350 lb, the IC4 and
            C6 to 330 lb, and the Wahoo smart bike to 250 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Price three years, not one.</strong> Use the table above with the prices on the day
            you buy. A bike that needs a $39 or $44 membership costs $1,404 or $1,584 more over three years
            than the same bike ridden without one.</li>
            <li><strong>Plan the space and the floor.</strong> A bike's footprint is small, but leave room to
            mount it and a mat underneath; our
            <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a> covers
            what to put down. Older or less steady riders may prefer the options in our
            <a href="/best-recumbent-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">recumbent bike guide</a>.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If you are weighing a bike against other cardio altogether, our
            <a href="/best-cardio-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home cardio machines guide</a>
            and <a href="/treadmill-vs-exercise-bike/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs exercise bike comparison</a>
            set the options side by side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best Peloton alternative?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Schwinn IC4 with Peloton App+. On 19 October 2026 the IC4 was $899 on Schwinn and App+ was $24 a month, about half the three-year cost of Peloton's cheapest bike with All-Access. If you want a large screen and automatic resistance, the NordicTrack X24 with iFIT is the closest Bike+ alternative.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use the Peloton App on a Schwinn IC4?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Schwinn lists the Peloton App among its compatible apps. Choose App+ at $24 a month, because App One limits you to three bike, tread or row classes a month. The app cannot read or set the IC4's resistance, so you follow the instructor's cues using the bike's own console.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much is Peloton App+ compared with All-Access?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On Peloton's site on 19 October 2026, App+ was $24 a month and the All-Access Membership was $44 a month; App One was $12.99. Over three years that is $864 for App+ against $1,584 for All-Access, a $720 difference. All-Access is required to use content on Peloton's own equipment.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Echelon a good Peloton alternative?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It looks like one, but OutdoorGearLab reports that Echelon bikes now work only when connected to Echelon's servers, tying the bike to its membership. That is more lock-in than Peloton, not less. If you want an open bike, an IC4 or Keiser works with apps you choose and keeps working without a subscription.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Peloton bike recalled?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two Peloton models have been recalled for seat posts that can break. The original Peloton Bike, model PL01, was recalled in May 2023, and the Original Series Bike+, model PL02 with serial numbers starting with T, in November 2025. Owners get a free replacement seat post. Peloton's current Cross Training Bike and Bike+ are not part of either recall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a smart trainer better than a Peloton?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cyclists who own a road bike, often yes: a trainer like the Wahoo KICKR CORE 2 ($549.99 on 19 October) sets its own resistance for workouts and virtual hills, which a Peloton bike cannot do in the same way, and you ride your own bike's fit. For non-cyclists who want classes, a Peloton or IC4 is simpler.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Choosing a Peloton Alternative</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">These are the ways people end up paying Peloton money for less than a Peloton.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing App One for a bike you ride every day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">App One is the cheapest Peloton tier, but Peloton limits it to three bike, tread or row classes a month, and a class counts once you have been in it for three minutes. If the bike is your main workout, you need App+. Check the current tier limits before you subscribe.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a closed bike to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A cheaper screen bike that only works with its maker's membership, or only when connected to its maker's servers, can cost more over its life than a Peloton and be worth less when you stop paying. Find out what the bike does with no membership before you buy, and add the fee for three to five years to the price.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a used Peloton without checking the seat post</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Millions of recalled Peloton bikes were sold, and many are resold. Before buying a used PL01 Bike or PL02 Bike+, check the model and serial number on the front fork and ask whether the free replacement seat post has been fitted. If the seller cannot say, contact Peloton before anyone rides it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the shoes and pedals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Peloton bikes use Delta-compatible cleats, the IC4 and C6 have SPD and toe-cage pedals, and some smart bikes ship with no pedals at all. If you already own cycling shoes, match the cleat or budget for new pedals, and do not assume shoes bought for one bike will clip into another.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

